The newest value is kept at the front of the linked list. A small sentinel node is used before the real top node, which makes the links easy to update when the first item is added or removed.

An empty stack returns `-1` from `pop` and `top`. Normal platform calls usually avoid those operations when the stack is empty, but the checks keep the class safe.

```java
class LinkedListStack {
    private static class Node {
        int value;
        Node next;

        Node(int value, Node next) {
            this.value = value;
            this.next = next;
        }
    }

    private final Node dummy;

    public LinkedListStack() {
        dummy = new Node(0, null);
    }

    public void push(int x) {
        dummy.next = new Node(x, dummy.next);
    }

    public int pop() {
        if (dummy.next == null) {
            return -1;
        }

        int value = dummy.next.value;
        dummy.next = dummy.next.next;
        return value;
    }

    public int top() {
        return dummy.next == null ? -1 : dummy.next.value;
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return dummy.next == null;
    }
}
```

`push`, `pop`, `top`, and `isEmpty` are all `O(1)`. The linked nodes need `O(n)` space.

A doubly linked list lets the stack use its tail as the top. New values go at the tail, and the previous pointer makes removal fast without walking through the list.

The first push and the last pop need special handling because both ends point to the same node. For example, pushing `3`, then `6`, then popping leaves an empty list after the value `6` is removed.

```go
type LinkedListStack struct {
    typeNode *stackNode
    head     *stackNode
    tail     *stackNode
}

type stackNode struct {
    value int
    prev  *stackNode
    next  *stackNode
}

func NewLinkedListStack() *LinkedListStack {
    return &LinkedListStack{}
}

func (s *LinkedListStack) push(x int) {
    node := &stackNode{value: x, prev: s.tail}

    if s.tail == nil {
        s.head = node
        s.tail = node
        return
    }

    s.tail.next = node
    s.tail = node
}

func (s *LinkedListStack) pop() int {
    if s.tail == nil {
        return -1
    }

    value := s.tail.value
    s.tail = s.tail.prev

    if s.tail == nil {
        s.head = nil
    } else {
        s.tail.next = nil
    }

    return value
}

func (s *LinkedListStack) top() int {
    if s.tail == nil {
        return -1
    }
    return s.tail.value
}

func (s *LinkedListStack) isEmpty() bool {
    return s.tail == nil
}
```

All four operations run in `O(1)` time. The two links per node make the implementation a little larger, but total space is still `O(n)`.

A stack only needs quick access to the newest item, so the head of a singly linked list works well as the top. Each `push` adds a node before the current head, and `pop` removes that same node.

Dry run: after pushing `4`, `7`, and `9`, the list is `9 -> 7 -> 4`. The next pop returns `9`.

```cpp
class LinkedListStack {
private:
    struct Node {
        int value;
        Node* next;

        Node(int value, Node* next = nullptr) : value(value), next(next) {}
    };

    Node* head;

public:
    LinkedListStack() : head(nullptr) {
    }

    void push(int x) {
        head = new Node(x, head);
    }

    int pop() {
        if (head == nullptr) {
            return -1;
        }

        Node* removed = head;
        int value = removed->value;
        head = head->next;
        delete removed;
        return value;
    }

    int top() {
        return head == nullptr ? -1 : head->value;
    }

    bool isEmpty() {
        return head == nullptr;
    }
};
```

Each operation takes `O(1)` time. The stack uses `O(n)` space, and removed nodes are deleted so there is no leftover memory.

The list here is circular and has a sentinel node. When the stack is empty, the sentinel points to itself. The real top is always stored just after the sentinel, so insertion and removal do not need separate first-node cases.

An empty stack is detected by checking whether `sentinel.next` is the sentinel. The `-1` return is only used for invalid empty `pop` or `top` calls.

```csharp
class LinkedListStack {
    private class Node {
        public int Value;
        public Node Next;

        public Node(int value) {
            Value = value;
        }
    }

    private readonly Node sentinel;

    public LinkedListStack() {
        sentinel = new Node(0);
        sentinel.Next = sentinel;
    }

    public void push(int x) {
        Node node = new Node(x);
        node.Next = sentinel.Next;
        sentinel.Next = node;
    }

    public int pop() {
        if (sentinel.Next == sentinel) {
            return -1;
        }

        Node removed = sentinel.Next;
        sentinel.Next = removed.Next;
        return removed.Value;
    }

    public int top() {
        return sentinel.Next == sentinel ? -1 : sentinel.Next.Value;
    }

    public bool isEmpty() {
        return sentinel.Next == sentinel;
    }
}
```

Every stack operation is `O(1)`. The sentinel is always present, and the stored values take `O(n)` space.

## Solution
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self,val,next=None):
        self.val=val
        self.next=next
class LinkedListStack:
    def __init__(self):
        self.head=None
    def push(self, x):
        element=Node(x)
        element.next=self.head
        self.head=element
    def pop(self):
        if not self.head:
            return -1
        value=self.head.val
        self.head=self.head.next
        return value

    def top(self):
        if not self.head:
            return -1
        return self.head.val

    def isEmpty(self):
        return not self.head```

